<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eric the Victorious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 995</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Eric VI of Sweden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eric the Victorious was a king of the Swedes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> in the late 10th century, the first Swedish king known with reasonable certainty. He won his name by defeating his nephew Styrbjörn the Strong at Fýrisvellir near Uppsala in the 980s. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a>, the first Christian king, ruled both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious.jpg" alt="Eric the Victorious" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MELLIN(1850)_p1.264_ERIK_SEGERSALL.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Eric is the first Swedish king in a consecutive succession who is attested in sources independent of each other, which is why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s list of rulers usually begins with him. His original territory lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppland">Uppland</a> and neighbouring provinces; the extent of his kingdom is unknown, but it may have reached down the Baltic coast as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>. The Flateyjarbok credits his success to an alliance with free farmers against an earl-class nobility. He probably introduced the system of universal conscription known as ledung in the provinces around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a>, and in all probability founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a>, where the first Swedish coins were later minted for his son Olof.</p><p>The sagas tell of his nephew Styrbjörn the Strong, said to be a son of Eric's brother Olof. When the Swedes refused to accept Styrbjörn as co-ruler, Eric gave him 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longship">longships</a>, and Styrbjörn became ruler of Jomsborg and an ally of the Danish king Harold Bluetooth, whose daughter Tyra he married. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> with an army and was defeated by Eric at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fyrisvellir">Battle of Fýrisvellir</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. According to one saga, Eric first sacrificed to Odin and promised to give himself to Odin in ten years if he won. Two skaldic verses by Thorvaldr Hjaltason describe the battle, and the runestones of Hällestad and Sjörup mention a battle at Uppsala, though they may be from the next century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The oldest narrative, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> around 1075, differs substantially from the sagas. It says Eric invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> against King Swein Forkbeard, who was forced to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and that Eric kept Denmark for a time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a> likewise says Eric ruled Denmark for seven years. Details of the conquest have been questioned, and the historian Peter Sawyer calls Adam's account a gross distortion, but Sture Bolin considers the conquest likely. Adam also reports that Eric, at first hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, accepted baptism and later reverted to the religion of his ancestors.</p><p>Adam's account places Eric's death between 992 and 995, and Snorri Sturlasson says he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. The sources name several wives, Sigrid, Świętosława, Gunhild and Aud, of whom two or three may be the same woman under different names. His only historically attested child is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a>, who died in 1022 and is considered the first ruler documented as accepted by both the Swedes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-vattern">Lake Vättern</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eric the Victorious” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Eric the Victorious</b>, who according to saga tradition won the Battle of Fyrisvellir near Uppsala around 985, is the first king whose rule over the Svear is reasonably certain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Eric the Victorious</b>, who defeated a rival at Fýrisvellir in the 980s, and his son Olof Skötkonung extended Svear rule over the Geats.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamla-uppsala">Gamla Uppsala</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.89,17.64&amp;year=985" aria-label="Gamla Uppsala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4920 156 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Eric the Victorious"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5370 426h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5370 426h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=59.89,17.64&amp;year=985">What was here in 985 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
